<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adela of Normandy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1067–<a href="/years/1137">1137</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Adela of Blois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adela of Normandy was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-count-of-blois">Stephen-Henry</a>, Count of Blois, and governed his lands while he was on the First Crusade and after his death in 1102. A patron of churches and poets, she was among the most capable rulers of her time. Her son Stephen became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1135, and another son, Henry, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adela-of-normandy.jpg" alt="Adela of Normandy" width="292" height="328" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adela_of_Normandy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adela was the youngest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-flanders">Matilda of Flanders</a>, and the favourite sister of King Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. She is generally believed to have been born between 1066 and 1070. Educated and high-spirited, she knew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and was probably schooled at a monastery, probably the Abbey of Sainte-Trinité in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>. With three older brothers and one younger, she could not inherit her father's honours, but her blood was a valuable asset in marriage.</p><p>Between 1080 and 1083, at about fifteen, she married Stephen Henry, son and heir of the count of Blois, at Chartres Cathedral. He was nearly twenty years her senior. The marriage linked the two most powerful families in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Stephen inherited Blois, Chartres and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meaux">Meaux</a> in 1089, and the couple had six or eight children who survived infancy. They decided matters together, and Adela helped lay the foundations of the county of Champagne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Stephen joined the First Crusade in 1096 with his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a>, and Adela acted as regent. She granted charters, corresponded with Ivo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>, toured the lands settling disputes and promoting economic growth, and commanded knights to go to battle with the king. Stephen's letters to her give an intimate view of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a>'s leaders. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orderic-vitalis">Orderic Vitalis</a>, when he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1100 she berated him constantly and urged him back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. He returned for the crusade of 1101 and was killed in a last stand after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> in 1102.</p><p>Adela continued as regent through the early rule of her son Thibaud until her retirement in 1120, and went on issuing charters and ruling jointly afterwards. In 1105, after Anselm visited her during an illness, she carried his threat of excommunication to Henry I. Orderic praised her as a wise and spirited woman. In 1107 she replaced her eldest son William as heir with Theobald. Her son Henry, pledged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluny-abbey">Cluny Abbey</a> at two as an oblate child, became Abbot of Glastonbury and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1120 Adela retired as a nun to the monastery at Marcigny, near her son Henry at Cluny, and kept in contact with her children and the churchmen of her former lands. That year her daughter Lucia-Mahaut drowned in the wreck of the White Ship. In 1111 her son Stephen had gone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to join Henry I's court, and on Henry's death in 1135 he seized the English throne in preference to Empress Matilda, beginning a civil war of nearly twenty years. Adela lived to see him king and died at Marcigny in 1137. She is a saint of the Roman Catholic Church, with a feast day on 24 February.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adela of Normandy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A poem by Baldric of Dol describes a similar hanging in the apartments of <b>Adela of Normandy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Stephen-Henry, husband of <b>Adela of Normandy</b>, was a leader of the First Crusade, and their son Stephen became king of England in 1135.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-blois">County of Blois</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Marcigny, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.44,1.1&amp;year=1112" aria-label="Rouen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4575 441 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adela of Normandy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5025 711h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5025 711h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.44,1.1&amp;year=1112">What was here in 1112? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
